From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 04:45:09 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 120/160] hjviewer: an installed PC keeps the incoming
 connections for 90 days (TV-W9)

TV-W9 (design w7-w9 3-3 and 5-3, D-16, critic 2-3): src/hjviewer_incoming.rs writes one line
when a connection is let in and one when it closes, in hjv-incoming.jsonl in the config folder
of an installed PC's service (an account build's service process, as for same-account logins;
nothing elsewhere), and folds them when read: the time, the peer's ID and the name it sent
(none for a staff row, S-05), the signed list's device name and masked owner for a same-account
row, verified or not as the connection manager's mark says (the one proof record, else the
staff mark), how it got in, the kind, the length and how it ended (on_close's reason). A start
without an end is "open" in the process that wrote it and "unknown" later; a reconnect of the
same session within two minutes of a lost connection continues its row. 90 days and 2,000 rows,
compacted at the first write and then daily. Refused attempts, addresses, passwords and the
session number never reach the answer.
The settings read it with main_get_common("hjv-incoming") (hjviewer_account::get_common, before
the account keys), which asks the service over the IPC (Data::Config "hjv-incoming", beside
trusted-devices) and gives {"error":"ipc"} without an answer. connection.rs gets one field and
three one-line hooks (after the audit line, the recent-session branch, on_close); the
uninstaller's elevated batch deletes the file beside the bound PC's ticket.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
